sequenceDiagram
  %% process.restart on the Gateway engine: the `process` family's `process.read_instance` and `process.restart`, a restart that runs at most once per Operation even when a receipt is lost (ADR 0267, ADR 0271; ProcessControlEngineFlow). Proved on a nested fleet by swamp/proof/process/restart.cases.yaml.
  %% A restart is not a desired state. The step carries evidence of the run it replaces (systemd's InvocationID, a random ID per run, or Docker's container ID and StartedAt) and records an attempt for its order before it calls the runtime. A changed value proves a restart happened; an unchanged value after an interrupted call proves nothing, so the step never restarts on that replay and reports the outcome unresolved.
  %% status: built
  %% command: process:restart
  %% kind: process.restart
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple
  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS
  participant N as Target Node<br/>executor
  CLI->>GW: process:restart 417
  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: provisioning
  %%> Same admission as process/restart.mmd
  opt drain-waiting, Vite or release plan
    Note over GW: Refuse the engine<br/>EngineUnsupported
    %%> - a restart stops first, so the drain-waiting Processes of process/engine/stop.mmd refuse here too, whatever their placement: a host Horizon unit (`GatewayHorizon::runs`, long-drain stop directives) and Gateway release-container Processes
    %%> - dev-server Processes (`vp-dev`, `vite`, Vite plans), Processes of an Instance outside app-dev (release layouts), and Instance-container Processes, as for process/engine/start.mmd
    %%> - decided at admission, before any order; the whole Operation takes the Swamp branch below
  end
  alt the engine admits the whole Operation
    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):
    %%> - `process.restart` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`
    %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: `process.read_instance` and `process.restart` (agent 0.33.0); a Node that reports only `process.ensure_state` takes the Swamp branch
    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order
    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start
    %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
    EN->>N: Read the running instance<br/>InvocationID · StartedAt
    %%> Step `read` (`process.read_instance`): read-only; the unit must be loaded; its `InvocationID`, empty while stopped (Docker: `<ID>@<State.StartedAt>` of the exact name). No boot ID: an InvocationID is random per run, so it never repeats across boots
    %%> Its receipt output `{"instance": …}` is the step's evidence; the restart step's input is built from that recorded output on every send, so a resend carries the same pinned value
    N-->>EN: Evidence<br/>output: instance
    EN->>N: Restart if unchanged<br/>compare, journal, restart
    %%> Step `restart` (`process.restart`), under the Process's target lock: first applies the admitted boot policy when it differs, as Swamp's `Set boot policy` does on every run (disabled for an on-demand unit); then, after the boot policy, reads the evidence again, so a run that changed meanwhile is left alone; only when it equals the pinned evidence, and no attempt of this order is recorded, does it write and sync the order's attempt file (`/var/lib/orbit/executor/process-restart/<operation>-<step>`, beside the journal; the process family owns it, so the shared executor journal gains no record type), and then run `systemctl restart` (Docker: `docker container restart`)
    %%> Evidence changed before any attempt (the unit restarted by itself since the read, or the Node rebooted): no restart; the receipt says `restarted: already` with the new evidence
    N-->>EN: New evidence<br/>active · new InvocationID
    %%> Verification read-back: loaded, active and the boot policy as admitted (a container: running with its admitted image), else `process.state_mismatch`; and an InvocationID (or ID and StartedAt) different from the pinned one, else `process.restart_unverified`
    %%> Identity and readiness are separate: after the decision the step reads the Process up to 30 times, 1 s apart, while the pinned run is still in place or the new run is still changing state (`activating`, `reloading`, `deactivating`; Docker `restarting`, `created`); an empty InvocationID is no run yet, not a new run; it never restarts again while it waits
    %%> The attempt file stays after the result, so the order stays spent across a crash or a reboot before `done` is durable, whatever the result was; files older than two days are pruned only after the executor's startup recovery has finished every open order, so an attempt stays while its order is recoverable, whatever the outage
    %%> The receipt's output is a JSON object with the members `instance` (the new run) and `restarted` (`now`, `already` or `recovered`); the executor writes them in key order and the Gateway accepts any member order
    opt the receipt is lost
      EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID
      %%> `done` in the journal: the stored result with `repeat: true`; nothing runs again
      %%> An attempt file without `done` (the executor died, or the Node rebooted, after the attempt), on a resend or on the executor's startup replay of the `open` order: it never restarts again; it waits up to 30 s for a verified new run, then reports `recovered`, else fails `process.restart_unresolved` (a stopped on-demand unit after a reboot keeps its empty InvocationID, so it fails unresolved and is not restarted again)
      %%> No record of the step: the first send never ran (an expired order left `executor.order_expired`), so the compare and restart run as above
      %%> Resends stop at the journal's replay window (`Engine::withinReplayWindow`): past it the Gateway sends nothing more, and Process 417 stays fenced by the Operation lock, awaiting a matching terminal receipt
      N-->>EN: Stored or recovered<br/>never a second restart
    end
    alt every step verified
      EN->>GW: Record Process active<br/>status · restarted
      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records
    else a step fails
      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail
      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it
      Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>desired state unchanged
      %%> - a failed `read` changed nothing; a failed `restart` left the unit as systemd reports it, and the retry is a new Operation with a fresh read
      %%> - the Operation records `operation.native_failed` with the Swamp step the phase stands for, from the step's own code: a failed `read`, and a restart's `process.boot_failed`, are `Set boot policy` (a container's read: `Restart the container`); a restart's `process.apply_failed` (its read before the restart or the restart itself), `process.unit_missing`, `process.container_missing` and `process.restart_unresolved` are `Restart the Process` / `Restart the container`; `process.read_failed` (a read after the restart) is `Check the unit` / `Check the container`; `process.state_mismatch` and `process.restart_unverified` are `Verify the state`
    end
  else the engine does not admit it
    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-process-control
    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-process-control (process/restart.mmd)
    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
  end
